      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much should I spend on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people, $900 to $1,500. That range covers every difference you will actually perceive — frame stability, deck length, a fold that works and a warranty in years. Spend less if you will only walk or are unsure the habit will hold; spend more only for a specific reason such as multiple daily users or high body weight.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a $500 treadmill worth buying?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For walking, yes — most treadmills are used at 2.5 to 4 mph regardless of price, and a budget machine does that competently. For running, no: decks at this price are 40 to 50 inches against the 60 a running stride needs, and frames are light enough to move under you.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the minimum I should spend to run on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "About $1,200 new, or $800 used. That is the floor for a 60-inch deck on a frame heavy enough to stay still at pace. Below it you shorten your stride to stay clear of the rear roller, which changes your gait and is the main reason treadmill running is said to feel unnatural.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I spend more if two people will use the treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually one tier up. Two people at three sessions each is 300 to 400 hours a year, which puts a mid-range DC motor into genuinely heavy use. Also buy for the taller user's stride — a long deck costs a shorter user nothing — and consider a tilting console if heights differ a lot.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I calculate whether a treadmill is worth the money?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Divide the total cost, subscription included, by the sessions you will realistically do in month six. A $1,200 machine used three times a week for five years is about $1.54 a session, which beats most gym memberships. The same machine used twice a month is $25 a session, which does not.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What should I spend the last $200 of my budget on?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Frame mass first, since it is the most perceptible difference between machines. Then deck length if it takes you from 55 to 60 inches and you run, then roller diameter from 1.9 to 2.5 inches. Screen size, top speed and decline are worth least. Reserve about $80 for a mat and lubricant regardless.",
          },
